django channels.
1. consumers - individual pieces that might handle chat messaging, or notifications - and tie them together with URL routing, protocol detection and other handy things to make a full application. (similar to django views)

2. scope. The scope is a set of details about a single incoming connection - such as the path a web request was made from, or the originating IP address of a WebSocket, or the user messaging a chatbot. The scope persists throughout the connection. For HTTP, the scope just lasts a single request. For WebSockets, it lasts for the lifetime of the socket

3. events. These represent user interactions - making a HTTP request, for example, or sending a WebSocket frame. we can have multiple of these in a connection.

4. handshake. web client sends an http request, and the server agrees to it, and the communicaiton upgrades from http to websocket.

5. Consumer. A consumer is the basic unit of Channels code. We call it a consumer as it consumes events, but you can think of it as its own tiny little application

6. flow of information. we have the client(browser which sends the requests. when it does so, the requests are received by the server.(gunicorn - wsgi, uvicorn,daphne - asgi). the server then uses an interface, ie asgi interface or wsgi interface to know how to call the application. ie asgi application. these are callables which know how to process your request. we call these consumers in channels.)
here's the flow.
    Client (browser/React)
       |
       | HTTP / WebSocket
       v
ASGI server (Daphne/Uvicorn)
  - Accepts TCP connection
  - Maintains connection
  - Manages async events
       |
       | ASGI interface
       v
Your ASGI / Channels application
  - Instance created per connection (scope)
  - Handles events (HTTP request, WS message)
  - Sends responses/messages back

7. channel layer - a low-level abstraction around a set of transports that allow you to send information between different processes. helps in interprocess communication.

8. A channel is a mailbox where messages can be sent to. Each channel has a name. Anyone who has the name of a channel can send a message to the channel.

9. A group is a group of related channels. A group has a name. Anyone who has the name of a group can add/remove a channel to the group by name and send a message to all channels in the group. It is not possible to enumerate what channels are in a particular group.

